# Agent instructions

This Roblox game uses KRF as its primary framework. For any gameplay work, use the KRF skill in `.agents/skills/krf/SKILL.md`, and build on what KRF provides instead of writing your own systems for it.

## Commands

- `npm run check` checks formatting, lint and types. Run it after every change. When it fails on formatting, run `npm run format`.
- `npm run build` builds <% if (options.places) { %>every place<% } else { %>the place<% } %>.
- `npm test`, `npm run test:studio` and `npm run dev` need Roblox Studio on Windows or macOS. When Studio isn't installed, run `check` and `build`, and say the Studio scripts were not run.
- `npm run dev` runs until it's stopped. Never run it as a command you wait for.
- Never run `npm run deploy:dev` or start a release or Deploy workflow unless the user asks. A publish replaces a live universe.

## Startup

- `src/server/Startup.server.luau` and `src/client/Startup.client.luau` call `KRF.Server.Init` and `KRF.Client.Init` once each. Never call `Init` anywhere else, and never register an Actor before the server's `Init` has returned.
- Start the game's own systems from those scripts after `Init` succeeds, with a module that has a `Start` function, as `PlayerActors` and `Controls` do.

## Catalog folders

- Every ModuleScript in a catalog folder, such as `src/server/Actions` or `src/server/Tags`, is loaded as a definition, subfolders included. Keep helpers outside catalog folders.

## World

<% if (options.mode === "hybrid") { -%>
- The world lives in `<%= name %>.rbxl`, which isn't in Git. Only code and packages are.
- Don't write world content into source. When a feature needs parts or models, build them from code at runtime or tell the user what to build in Studio.
<% } else { -%>
- World content is stored as Rojo files in `world/`<% if (options.places) { %>, with a folder for each place<% } %>. `npm run dev` syncs it into Studio, and each Studio save is captured back into `world/`.
- Studio work that hasn't been saved and captured isn't in source, so it won't be built or published.
<% } -%>

## Tests

- `npm test` runs Jest in Studio without pressing Play. The startup scripts don't run and there are no players.
- Each spec calls `KRF.Server._ResetForTests()` and `KRF.Server.Init` with the catalogs it needs in `beforeEach`, registers its own Models, and unregisters them, destroys them and resets KRF again in `afterEach`. Never call `_ResetForTests` from game code.
- Pass the game's real catalogs, such as `require(ServerScriptService.Game.Resources)`, so specs check the definitions the game ships.
- Exit code 1 means a spec failed, 6 means no specs or no Jest config, and 7 means Studio couldn't run the tests, which isn't a test failure.
- Specs cover server rules. For client code, input and UI, tell the user what to try in a Studio play session.

## Code quality

- Keep `--!strict` in every module. Type every parameter, return value and table that crosses a module boundary, and fix type errors instead of silencing them with `:: any`.
- When other modules need a module's types, export them with `export type` and require them from that module. Never redeclare a type in a second place.
- When logic is needed in more than one place, write it once in a shared module with a clear, typed function, and require it instead of copying it.
- Put constants, such as damage, ranges and durations, in their own module and require them where they're used, instead of repeating literal values. Keep that module outside catalog folders.

## Packages and versions

- Never edit `Packages` or `DevPackages`. Wally rewrites them on every `npm install`.
- Don't change the KRF version in `wally.toml` or the `krf-tools` version in `package.json` as part of a feature. Commit `wally.lock`.
